\title{A view of a text can drop its first bytes without an option}
\taxon{Change}
\date{2026-11-17}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{changelog}
\tag{stdlib}
\tag{unreleased}
\meta{release}{Unreleased}
\meta{pr}{1070}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\code{\verb~|TextView::drop_byte(count)~} is new: the view without its first \code{\verb~|count~} bytes, and the empty view at the end when the view has fewer. Like \code{\verb~|slice_byte~} and \code{\verb~|tail_byte~} it counts bytes and does not ask for a character boundary. \code{\verb~|Automaton::longest~} uses it for the text left after a match, where it took a boxed option of a view apart; one call of \code{\verb~|longest~} runs about a quarter fewer instructions. See [the session](ses-A1OD).}

\p{Merged as \pr{1070}.}
